    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21651",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = [3, \\ 1]^T.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = [-1, \\ 0]^T$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$  Sketch the phase portrait of the system.",
      "worked_solution": "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/61321eab37e434737b898844b503f4c1177c03863113a7f17869bdaab693ad91.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/847700025fe37b6e9fc8e42de4989d01e49416b75987afced9b1b8d4969be629.svg) are equilibrium solutions of the system since $\\mathbf{x}'_E(t) = \\mathbf{0}.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\dfrac13x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/bfc7b00f7f63aec120179da3f89f72f0aa53f13d299c95ed8f53e6238d329e51.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/1674ba4c9d5fff5c037421709107505482a12de4711a3fcbb47ccae52ec32a11.svg)\nFor example:\n\n- When $c_1=1$ and $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t+2 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/55dfdfce480deba74fe46c1634e4c7ffb711fbd617525cdb5387bdabfc83ae75.png)\n\n- When $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t-2 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/2fb2297c741ea20ca729dcacb9934d7f8d1cca58af93c2b75db177890934b2b6.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14226345/assets/9e7970ba2d6bbe4745ee498cdbc72f2b7d8689219291847644bd6483696cc456.png)",
